# Every text file is stored LF and checked out LF, on every platform.
#
# Without this the working copy's line endings come from whatever each developer
# has core.autocrlf set to, and that is not a cosmetic difference here: a C# raw
# string literal takes the line endings of the file it is written in. A fixture
# written as one therefore means something different depending on how the
# repository was cloned. NoteAnchoringTests compares source lines against anchor
# text that Broiler.Code.Review normalizes to LF, so five of those tests failed
# on every Windows checkout while CI, checking out LF, stayed green — a test that
# cannot fail on the machine the code is written on is not protecting anything.
#
# text=auto rather than a bare text: git's own binary detection decides what gets
# converted, so a binary added later is left alone rather than corrupted by a
# rule written before it existed. Every file tracked here today is text, and
# there is deliberately no list of binary extensions for files this repository
# does not have — the same measure-first rule the build warnings in
# Directory.Build.props are written under.
#
# The component submodules are separate repositories with their own working
# copies. This file does not reach them.
* text=auto eol=lf
